/*
================================================================================
CUSTOM COROUTINE SCOPE — THEORY (COMPLETE & RE-ARRANGED)
================================================================================

1️⃣ What is a Custom CoroutineScope?
-----------------------------------
- A Custom CoroutineScope means:
  → YOU create the scope manually
  → YOU control its lifecycle

- Android does NOT manage this scope for you.


2️⃣ Difference from lifecycleScope / viewModelScope
---------------------------------------------------
- lifecycleScope / viewModelScope:
  ✔ Automatically managed by Android
  ✔ Cancelled when lifecycle ends

- Custom CoroutineScope:
  ❌ NOT lifecycle-aware
  ❌ YOU must cancel it manually


3️⃣ When should we use a Custom CoroutineScope?
-----------------------------------------------
Use Custom CoroutineScope when:
- Code is OUTSIDE Activity / Fragment / ViewModel
- Repository classes
- Manager / Helper / SDK classes
- Background logic classes
- When you want FULL control over coroutine lifetime


4️⃣ Risk of Custom CoroutineScope
--------------------------------
❌ If you forget to cancel it → MEMORY LEAK

Because:
- Coroutine keeps running
- References remain in memory

RULE:
Custom Scope = Manual Start + Manual Cancel


================================================================================
5️⃣ How to Create a New CoroutineScope (CORRECT WAY)
================================================================================

To create a NEW CoroutineScope, we must provide a CoroutineContext.

CoroutineContext = Dispatcher + Job

Example:
*/
private val scope = CoroutineScope(Dispatchers.Main + Job())

/*
================================================================================
6️⃣ WHY do we need BOTH Dispatchers.Main AND Job()?
================================================================================

CoroutineScope needs TWO things:

1) Dispatcher → WHERE the coroutine runs
2) Job        → HOW LONG the coroutine lives

------------------------------------------
Dispatchers.Main
------------------------------------------
- Runs coroutine on Main (UI) Thread
- Required for UI updates (TextView, Button, etc.)
- Without dispatcher → coroutine has no thread

------------------------------------------
Job()
------------------------------------------
- Controls lifecycle of coroutine
- Enables cancellation
- Acts as parent for child coroutines

------------------------------------------
Why BOTH are required?
------------------------------------------
When creating a scope manually, Kotlin asks:

❓ Where should this coroutine run?
❓ Who owns it and who cancels it?

So YOU must explicitly answer BOTH.
*/


/*
================================================================================
7️⃣ What happens if we DON’T pass these parameters?
================================================================================

❌ INVALID:
CoroutineScope()

WHY?
- No Dispatcher → no thread info
- No Job → no lifecycle control

Kotlin enforces this to prevent unsafe,
uncontrolled coroutines.
*/


/*
================================================================================
8️⃣ Why don’t we pass Dispatcher + Job for GlobalScope?
================================================================================

GlobalScope already HAS an internal CoroutineContext.

Conceptual internal definition:

GlobalScope = CoroutineScope(
    Dispatchers.Default + SupervisorJob()
)

So when you write:
GlobalScope.launch { }

You are automatically using:
- Dispatcher → Dispatchers.Default
- Job → SupervisorJob

That is why you DON'T pass them manually.
*/


/*
================================================================================
9️⃣ Comparison — Custom CoroutineScope vs GlobalScope
================================================================================

Custom CoroutineScope
--------------------
- YOU create it
- YOU provide Dispatcher
- YOU provide Job
- YOU cancel it manually
- Risk of memory leak if misused

GlobalScope
-----------
- Kotlin provides it
- Dispatcher already set
- Job already set
- Lives as long as app process
- Dangerous for most Android cases
*/


